We introduce the nonlocal constant of motion (NCOM) for the d-dimensional case for the higher-order system described by the equation:
\begin{equation}\label{eq:main}
\frac{d^s}{dt^s}y_a = f_{a} (y, \frac{d}{dt}y, \dots, \frac{d^{n-1}}{dt^{n-1}}y),
\end{equation}
where $n \geq 1$ and $s \geq 1$. For any $s \leq n$, NCOMs are defined as specific functionals of the trajectory $y$ and its derivatives up to order $n-s$. These nonlocal quantities remain constant along solutions to Eq.~\eqref{eq:main}. When $s=n$, the functional reduces to the standard action, which is well-established to be invariant. For orders $s \leq n$, NCOMs are derived directly from the system's Lagrangian. Furthermore, NCOMs are unique up to an additive constant and can be efficiently computed using the action and its associated canonical momenta.

